            <ArticleTitle>Reactive Arthritis Caused by Urinary Tract Infection.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1195-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2169/internalmedicine.55.6112</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report the case of a 58-year-old man presenting with chest pain who underwent percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI). The patient subsequently developed a fever over 38°C, pain on micturition, and cloudy urine 3 days following PCI. Urine cultures were positive for Escherichia coli and Enterococcus faecalis, whereas blood cultures were negative. Arthritis occurred two weeks following urinary tract infection (UTI). We herein present a rare case of reactive arthritis caused by UTI following PCI.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
